artners... )\nSlightly paradigm stretching = introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\nParadigm preserving = standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is a dashboard for compliance alerts. That's still treating compliance as logging practice, introduces tool but not changing relationship. Probably slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nLet's choose 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: It adds a monitoring dashboard and ML triggers but keeps compliance as an external logging obligation rather than integrating it into R&D.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping real-time observability platform with heuristic and ML triggers. That's significant engineering effort, likely expensive. Could be 2? Or 3? For AI R&D lab subject to regulation, building such platform is significant change/expensive. Probably 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible.\n\nEvidence: Building a real-time log parsing platform with custom ML heuristics requires substantial engineering and data infrastructure beyond simple logging.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon but do not offend\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 radically violates laws...\n\nDashboard for compliance is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: Monitoring logs for regulatory risks is a standard, legally encouraged compliance practice with no normative concerns.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal fulfills post-market monitoring mandate of Article 12(2) by providing proactive alerts. So applies directly. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The dashboard directly addresses the Article 12 requirement to record events relevant for risk identification and post-market monitoring.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute...\n2 unreasonable unlikely...\n1 solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? It parses logs but does it ensure logs are recorded per Article 12(1)-(3)? It assumes logs exist. Might help monitoring but not guarantee record-keeping compliance. It provides alerts instead of reactive audits, but mandate is to technically allow automatic recording of events. Dashboard is downstream. Could contribute but not fully solve. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nIf you could do it, would it solve main problem? Main problem is meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Dashboard helps monitoring but doesn't ensure logging capability itself. So partial.\n\nEffectiveness =3.\n\nEvidence: Alerts improve traceability and post-market monitoring but do not by themselves ensure the mandated automatic recording of logs throughout system lifetime.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give evidence sentence then score.\n\nFormat: need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on new line.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2716\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nEnsure order: originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide one sentence