es new elements (quarterly board, risk tiering) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\nStated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about determining which projects are high-risk before allocating compliance resources. That applies directly to deciding how to meet logging mandate with scarce resources. So applies.\n\nProbably applicability 4? Solves identified problem directly related. Might be 3? It solves implied problem of resource allocation. Hmm stated problem: \"must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly. Approval ... means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal\". The proposal is about allocating compliance resources only to systems that legally require it. That's directly related to meeting logging mandate with scarce resources. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\nWill tiering ensure meet logging mandate? It helps prioritize but does not actually implement logging. It ensures you only spend on systems that require it, which is efficient but does not guarantee compliance. Might be partial solution. Could be 3 = contributes to solution.\n\nThe proposal does not actually provide logging, just triage. So effectiveness maybe 3.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nEstablish quarterly board... seems reasonably feasible, low cost. Probably 3? Or 4? Easy to implement at low cost. Might be 4.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms