sses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis is standardized library, middleware hooks. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools/processes but compliance remains separate? It reduces burden but doesn't change relationship fundamentally. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... Common, mundane, boring =1. Interesting=2. Unusual interesting=3. Not expressed before AND ingenious=4.\n\nStandardized logging framework middleware is common. Probably common, mundane. 1.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. 4.\n\nImplementability: degree to which can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible=3. Significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible=2. Totally infeasible=1.\n\nDeveloping middleware layer hooking into inference pipelines, integrate with version control. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Maybe 3.\n\nLet's output JSON? Instruction: Return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P2263\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 4\n...\n\nDo in order.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The unified library automates event recording, timestamping and input-output capture across high-risk projects, directly meeting Article 12's automatic logging and traceability mandate. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: It targets the lab's need to meet Article 12 logging requirements while reducing researcher burden, which is the stated compliance decision problem. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice layered onto unchanged R&D workflows. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: A standardized middleware logging library for AI compliance is a common, mundane industry response to regulatory logging mandates. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building internal logging middleware that captures metadata without altering models complies with law and offends no sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating a middleware layer that hooks inference pipelines and integrates with version control requires engineering effort but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
