ing framework is fairly expected. I'd say common, mundane -> 1. But it mentions immutable ledger maybe a bit unusual. Let's pick 2 = interesting.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is compliant logging. Acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping modular API hooking into model inference and training pipelines across all lab projects, immutable ledger. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Probably 2? Could be 3 some changes reasonably feasible. Developing framework is significant engineering effort. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal ensures legal compliance while minimizing manual burden. Captures timestamps, input hashes, database references. Article 12 requires logs of events, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... Framework captures timestamps, input hashes, database references. Might be partial. Could solve logging mandate. Probably 4 = will solve stated problem? It automates compliant logs across all lab projects. Seems reasonable and will solve. I'd give 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied...\nIt directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id, for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Ins