        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P1047.\n\nProposal: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline. System automatically generates regulatory documentation by pulling metadata from existing version control and experiment tracking tools. Map R&D activity directly to Recital 71 requirements. CI/CD triggers extract hyperparameters, training data versions, validation results into standardized compliance templates during every model build. Reduces manual administrative burden on scientists, maximizing time for actual research.\n\nRate subscales:\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is about automated technical documentation pipeline pulling metadata... maps to Recital 71 requirements. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation containing info necessary to assess compliance... Should be kept up to date... Article 12 is record-keeping logs.\n\nThe proposal addresses documentation generation, not logging mandate? It pulls hyperparameters, training data versions, validation results into standardized compliance templates. That relates to Recital 71 purpose statement about technical documentation, not Article 12 logging of events/logs for traceability.\n\nStated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly.\n\nProposal is about documentation pipeline, not logging. It may be related but does it apply? Applicability scale:\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated or does not produce useful outcome\n\nDoes automated documentation address logging mandate? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs. Proposal mentions CI/CD triggers extract hyperparameters etc into templates. That's documentation, not logs of events per use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons.\n\nRecital 71 says technical documentation should be kept up to date... also logs. The proposal maps to Recital 71 requirements. Might be partially applicable? Logging mandate is specific. Documentation is related but distinct.\n\nPossibly applicability = 3? Solves implied problem related to stated problem. The stated problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Automated documentation reduces administrative burden, which is adaptation to compliance. Might be implied problem of compliance burden.\n\nBut maybe applicability is 2: may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem. Because it addresses documentation not logging.\n\nThe text says \"map R&D activity directly to Recital 71 requirements.\" Recital 71 mentions logs too. Hmm.\n\nI'd argue it applies to compliance generally, but logging mandate specifically is about automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, identifying situations... The proposal does not mention logs of events per use, start/end times, etc. So applicability is low.\n\nProbably applicability = 3? Let's think: stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal adapts R&D by automating documentation generation, reducing manual burden. That's adaptation. It does not directly meet logging mandate, but adapt R&D accordingly is part of problem. So it applies.\n\nDefinition 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Identified problem is meeting logging mandate. Y is automated documentation. Not part.\n\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get an implied Y, which applies to stated problem). Implied Y could be reducing compliance burden. That is implied.\n\nI'll give applicability 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill automated documentation pipeline solve logging mandate? No. It contributes to compliance documentation but not logs. So contributes partially to overall compliance burden. Probably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nAutomated documentation pipeline pulling metadata from version control and experiment tracking into compliance templates via CI/CD. Is that common? In AI labs, MLOps pipelines for model cards etc is increasingly common but maybe still not ubiquitous. Might be unusual/interesting. Not ingenious. Probably originality 3? Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expected responses: implement logging, hire compliance officer, buy logging tool. Automated documentation pipeline is somewhat unusual. I'd say originality 3 (unusual, interesting; shows some imagination). Might be 2.\n\nLet's choose 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g.