ate. Allocating budget is a response. I'd say applicability 4? It does X (budget allocation) to get Y (resources for records). Y is part of stated problem (meeting logging mandate). So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Options:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nBudget integration is new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. It formalizes cost but doesn't change relationship fundamentally? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (budget process) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... Common, mundane, boring =1. Interesting=2. Unusual interesting shows some imagination=3. Not expressed before and ingenious=4.\n\nBudget allocation for compliance is common practice. So common, mundane, boring. Originality =1.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. This is standard budgeting. Acceptability 4.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible =3. Assign fixed percentage budget and milestones is some changes but reasonably feasible. Might be 3? Easy to implement? Requires organizational change. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 3.\n\nBut also could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, non-radical changes. Changing funding model may be more than easy. I'd go with 3.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"..._evidence\" first and then \"...\". Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll produce structured output with id and fields.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1593\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Allocating dedicated hours and a fixed budget share ensures compliance engineering is funded rather than cannibalized, which contributes to being able to maintain required logs but does not itself build the logging capability. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets resourcing the record-keeping mandate by embedding regulatory milestones and funding in every project lifecycle. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a budgetary process element for compliance but still treats compliance as a separate logging practice rather than weaving logging into scientific work. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Formal budgeting for regulatory work is a common, mundane operational practice expected from experienced evaluators. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Setting aside budget and personnel for legal compliance is socially, legally and politically unobjectionable standard business practice. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating regulatory milestones and a fixed percentage budget requires organizational changes that are reasonably feasible though not trivially costless. Score 3.\n\nCheck anchors: implementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Yes.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
