tabase, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. Proposal mentions mapping to specific version and logging hashes alongside each input match, removes manual docs. Does not explicitly mention period of use start/end time, or identification of natural persons. Might be partial.\n\nIt says technically fulfills strict record-keeping requirements for Annex III systems. If we take at face value, it would solve. But evidence shows only part. The rating should be based on idea content. It does not mention period logging or person identification. So maybe contributes but not full. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nEvidence: It automates provenance for database version and input matches but does not explicitly log usage timestamps or verifier identities required by Article 12(3)(a) and (d).\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nIntegrate existing data versioning tools directly into runtime environment to log unique dataset hashes alongside each input match. Using existing tools suggests reasonably feasible. Might be some changes. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Reusing existing data versioning tools in runtime is a reasonably feasible engineering integration with moderate changes.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nThis is standard compliance logging, acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Automated immutable provenance logging aligns with regulatory expectations and does not violate legal or business norms.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? The instruction says give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could output as list.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P2553\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparad