g: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged =1. Hmm.\n\nThe description: 4 = paradigm breaking... 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners... ) 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nAudit process is standard compliance. I'd say 1. Might be 2. Let's think: It introduces audit process for resource allocation. That's a new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly stretching. Probably 2? The instrument says slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits.\n\nEffectiveness: degree idea will solve problem.\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill audit solve logging mandate? It helps allocate budget efficiently but doesn't actually implement logging. Effectiveness 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). It doesn't meet logging mandate itself, just allocates resources. So contributes partially. Score 3.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves an identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Audit allocates resources for logging. That's directly related. So applicability 4? Might be 3 solves implied problem. I'd say 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related.\n\nLet's output format: need echo id, then for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subsc