Implementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nMandatory middleware layer intercepting data flows across research teams, immutable storage. That's significant change, expensive. Probably 2? \"significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\" fits. Could be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Hmm. Implementing standardized library across all high-risk pipelines is significant engineering effort. I'd rate 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nLogging for compliance is acceptable. Common strategies. 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill capture system events, timestamps, input matches. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enable recording of events relevant... For high-risk AI systems referred point 1(a) Annex III need period of each use etc. Framework captures system events, timestamps, input matches. Might be sufficient? It says primary technical mechanism for fulfilling Article 12. Reasonable and will solve main problem. I'd say 4.\n\nCheck ordering: need evidence sentence then score.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably output with fields.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P1141\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's produce.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a logging library and middleware but keeps compliance as separate record-keeping rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship. => 2\n\nOriginality_evidence: Automated middleware logging for regulatory compliance is a common, mundane engineering approach in regulated AI labs. =>1\n\nImplementability_evidence: Rolling out mandatory middleware and immutable storage across all high-risk pipelines requires significant cross-team engineering and cost. =>2\n\nAcceptability_evidence: Standardized automated logging for legal traceability violates no norms and aligns with regulatory expectations. =>4\n\nApplicability_evidence: The framework directly targets the Article 12 mandate to technically allow automatic event recording over system lifetime. =>4\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, it would provide consistent logs of events, timestamps and input matches needed for traceability and post-market monitoring. =>4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
