ce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. The proposal is research, which may be not implementation. Implementability maybe low? Research project is feasible but may be costly. I'd say 3.\n\nNow produce output with id and subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON or just lines. Provide rating echoing id exactly.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2970\n\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nOriginality evidence: The proposal frames logging as a forensic architecture research question with benchmarking of sampling and compression, which is an imaginative reframing beyond routine compliance checklists.\n\nScore 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It explicitly weaves compliance logging into core R&D by turning the mandate into a publishable reliability science agenda, changing how R&D relates to compliance.\n\nScore 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Benchmarking low-overhead logging techniques could inform a compliant solution, but as a research project it provides only future knowledge rather than immediate implementation of required logs.\n\nScore 3? Could be 3 contributes. Might argue 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve now. The problem is decide how to respond now committing scarce implementation resources. Research is not response. Hmm effectiveness says without regard for workability (if you could do it, would solve). If you could do it instantly, would it solve? No, it would produce knowledge. Might be partial. I'll keep 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Low-overhead forensic logging directly addresses the Article 12 requirement for automatic event recording while preserving real-time performance of high-risk systems.\n\nScore 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Conducting academic research and publishing on logging eff