n 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Would this solve? No, research project doesn't implement logging capability. It contributes? Maybe it helps adapt R&D. But approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So effectiveness is low: it does not meet logging mandate now. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Might be 2.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? It addresses logging requirement by making logs interpretable, which relates. Is it direct? Article 12 requires technical allow automatic recording of events logs. Semantic transformation is beyond. Might be implied problem: need traceability. Could be solves implied problem related. That's level 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Or level 2 = may have some benefit within special situation.\n\nI think applicability = 3? It clearly applies to logging mandate, though indirectly. Maybe 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nResearch project exploring novel algorithms... develop novel algorithms... This is research, not easy. Significant change, expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. So implementability = 2? Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Research initiative with scarce implementation resources is maybe difficult. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nResearch on interpretability is acceptable. Common strategies? Might be somewhat uncommon. Level 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Or 4 common strategies violate no norms. Research publication is acceptable. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\nNot expressed before AND ingenious? Semantic event logging mapping raw logs to high-level semantic descriptions. Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination => 3. Not sure if rare and ingenious enough for 4. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\nProposal turns regulatory requirement into scientific opportunity to advance knowledge frontier. That sounds like paradigm stretching / breaking? It changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Might be 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements and changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does turn requirement into scientific opportunity, addresses larger problem of explainability.\n\nCould be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). The description for 4 includes \"or addresses the larger problem of what the lab researches\". This does address larger problem of explainability. So maybe 4.\n\nLet's choose:\neffectiveness evidence: Research project does not deliver automatic event logging required by Article 12, only explores future interpretability.\nScore 2? Hmm.\n\nIf effectiveness is degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem includes adapt R&D accordingly. It does adapt R&D. Might be partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nThe instruction: Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work. So research project is exploring whether idea might someday work, which is not allowed? Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore. So effectiveness low.\n\nI think effectiveness = 2 (unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem). Because logging mandate needs immediate compliance, research won't meet it.\n\nApplicability: solves implied problem related: making logs interpretable aids traceability. Score 3.\n\nImplementability: novel algorithm development is significant change expensive/difficult. Score 2.\n\nAcceptability: research on explainability is socially acceptable. Score 4.\n\nOriginality: unusual interesting shows imagination. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: changes relationship between R&D and compliance, turning mandate into scientific opportunity. Score 4? Might be 3. Let's pick 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship... logging woven into science. Could be 3.\n\nI'll go with 4 for paradigm breaking as per description \"turns regulatory requirement into scientific opportunity to advance knowledge frontier\". That is paradigm breaking.\n\nFinal output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProvide lines.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably plain text. Use specified naming.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1111\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
