nd decision logs across all high-risk models, unified API integrates with training and deployment pipelines to log input data, reference databases, verification timestamps without manual intervention. Article 12 requires logging capabilities for identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation; for point 1(a) systems minimum recording period of use, reference DB, input data for match, identification natural persons involved in verification. This proposal covers those. Seems would solve. So effectiveness 4.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is automated logging. Acceptable. No violation. Common. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nDeveloping unified API integrating directly with training and deployment pipelines across all high-risk models is significant engineering effort, not easy low cost. Probably significant change, expensive/difficult. So implementability 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" could be 3. Developing standardized software layer is non-trivial but feasible for an AI lab. Might be significant change. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nAlternatively could be 3. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide: implementability 2? The proposal says develop unified API integrates directly with pipelines to log without manual intervention. That's non-radical? It is radical for R&D lab. Might be significant change.\n\nI'll pick:\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: The framework embeds logging into training and deployment pipelines and repurposes compliance data for research failure analysis, changing how R&D relates to compliance.\nScore 3