uld be kept up to date. So it's directly related.\n\nI think applicability = 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem: do X (living doc) to get Y (audit trail for regulators during post-market monitoring). Y is part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Post-market monitoring is mentioned in Article 12(2)(b). So yes.\n\nMaybe 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring =1\nInteresting =2\nUnusual interesting shows some imagination =3\nNot expressed before AND ingenious... =4\n\nLiving technical document, version-controlled documentation mapping algorithm versions to datasets... This is fairly standard compliance practice. Not surprising. Might be common. Could be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting.\n\nExperienced evaluator would expect labs to give responses about documentation protocols. Probably common. I'd say originality = 1 (common, mundane).\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces living document and version control. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It replaces static reports with dynamic records. That's new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2.\n\nOr 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Living document might be slight change.\n\nI think 2 = sligh