blem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal only addresses one logging item (3d). Might be partial solution. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but it is only a partial solution). Likely 3.\n\nEvidence: It provides verifiable identity logging for the specific Annex III verification requirement but does not address broader automatic event logging, traceability, or other Article 12 items.\n\nScore 3.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nBuilding dedicated UI dashboard with encrypted database logging unique user IDs timestamps. For R&D lab? Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivial but not huge. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Building a verification dashboard and encrypted audit log requires software development and integration with existing search pipeline but uses standard UI and logging practices.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nLogging user IDs for compliance is common. Acceptable. Might be 4.\n\nEvidence: Tracking verifier identity for regulatory compliance is a standard, non-controversial practice that aligns with legal traceability expectations.\n\nScore 4.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nProposal is human-in-the-loop verification interface. That's common. Probably common mundane boring =1. Or interesting? Might be interesting? The instrument: 4 = rare AND ingenious, 3= unusual interesting shows some imagination, 2=interesting,1=common mundane boring. This is fairly standard compliance UI. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Human-in-the-loop verification dashboards with identity logging are a common, routine compliance solution in high-risk AI systems.\n