ble measures. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Testing for compliance is common. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Yes.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nTest suite is new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools...) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 interesting. 1 common mundane boring.\n\nValidation test suite for regulatory logging is fairly common practice (shift-left compliance). Might be interesting but not rare. Could be 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common mundane boring. Test suites are common. Probably 2? Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Testing compliance is fairly expected. Maybe common mundane. I'd give 2? Let's pick 2 = interesting (maybe somewhat uncommon). Could be 1 common. The idea of integration tests for logging is pretty standard software engineering. Might be common mundane boring => 1.\n\nThe anchored levels: originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. I think test suite is common. So 1.\n\nLet's output format: need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on new line.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProvide:\n\nid P1041\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal creates automated integration tests that verify Article 12 logging fields before deployment, directly addressing how the lab meets the mandatory record-keeping requirement. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It will catch compliance gaps early but does not itself build the required logging capabilities or cover all lifetime traceability needs. Score 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building simulated high-risk event tests and CI/CD checks requires modest engineering effort and existing testing infrastructure. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Testing for regulatory compliance before release is a standard, norm-conforming software practice. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The suite adds new validation tools while still treating compliance as separate logging practice rather than changing the R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Automated compliance test suites are a common, mundane software engineering practice in regulated development. Score 1.\n\nReturn accordingly.",
