\nProposal specifically satisfies Article 12(3)(d). It addresses logging mandate for human verification attribution. That is part of logging mandate. Does it solve whole problem? Logging mandate includes many items a,b,c,d and also general logging. This only covers one sub-requirement. Might be partial solution. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Could be 4 if solves stated problem without regard for workability? Stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Meeting just one clause may not fully meet mandate. Probably partial. So 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\nThe proposal does X to get Y where Y is logging human verification identity which is part of Article 12 requirement. Directly related. So applicability 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating, echo its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely produce lines.\n\nWe can output as JSON object with fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2262\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nProvide concise one sentence.\n\nEnsure order listed: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The interface enforces identity logging for human reviewers as required by EU law and does not violate privacy norms beyond compliance. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Adding an authentication gateway and UI logging to an existing verification workflow requires moderate software changes but uses standard authentication and logging compone