ernal audit directly targets Article 12's required log contents for high-risk systems.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Build validation engine to scan logs periodically. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Maybe moderate. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: Building a log-scanning validator requires engineering effort but uses existing log data and standard software practices.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Internal auditing is common. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: Proactive compliance checking is a standard, non-controversial risk-management practice.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious... Automated Compliance Verification Auditor. Is this common? Probably interesting but not ingenious. Originality scale: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious; 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination; 2 interesting; 1 common mundane boring. This seems fairly common. Might be 2 = interesting. Or 1 common? Many labs do compliance checks. I'd say 2 interesting.\n\nEvidence: Log validation tools are familiar in regulated industries, offering little imaginative surprise.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Scale: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship e.g., logging woven into science... 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab way of working unchanged.\n\nThis is just standard compliance tool. It's paradigm preserving? It introduces new element (auditor) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools... but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way of working unchanged.\n\nIt adds internal audit tool, maybe slightly stretching. I'd pick 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a validation tool yet keeps R&D separate from compliance, treating logging as an add-on practice.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first then \"...\". Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1965\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide final.",
