on (Recital 71) but Article 12 is about logging events. However documentation pipeline may help with record-keeping? It doesn't explicitly address automatic recording of events logs for system use. Might partially contribute. Could be partial solution: helps with technical documentation, reduces burden, but does not directly implement event logging mandated in Art12(1)-(3). So effectiveness = 3? reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Could be 3.\n\nAlternatively effectiveness might be 2 because it addresses documentation not logging mandate. Article 12 is record-keeping logs. Proposal mentions auto-generates compliance reports, metadata tagging, dashboard compile into required comprehensive form for regulators. It doesn't mention logging events of system use. So maybe solves unrelated problem? No, it's related to compliance but mis-targets.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal addresses documentation burden, not logging mandate per se. Might be partially effective? It contributes to compliance burden reduction but doesn't ensure log of events. Could be effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute.\n\nLet's choose 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt clearly applies? It does apply to compliance burden, but Article 12 specifically about logging. The proposal talks about technical documentation pipeline, which is Recital 71. Might be directly related. I think applicability = 3? Solves implied problem of administrative burden. Could be 4? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Automated documentation helps meet record-keeping. Hmm.\n\nMaybe applicability = 3: solves implied problem related (administrative burden). I'd give 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon but does not offend\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates\n\nAutomated documentation is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes or reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrating metadata tagging into Git repositories, central dashboard compiling metadata... Requires engineering effort. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not trivial easy low cost. So 3.\n\nLet's output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably need JSON? The instruction says Return rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain lines.\n\nI'll output structured.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2086\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2? Let's decide final.\n\nI think paradigm_relatedness = 2 slightly paradigm stretching. Because introduces new elements (tools, formats) but still treats compliance as logging practice. It does not change relationship fundamentally, just automates.\n\nOriginality = 2\n\nEffectiveness = 3\n\nApplicability = 3\n\nAcceptability =4\n\nImplementability =3\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The pipeline adds automated metadata tagging and dashboards but keeps compliance as a separate documentation task rather than reshaping R&D or compliance relationships. => score 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Auto-generating reports from version control is a familiar DevOps practice applied to AI compliance, making it interesting but not unusual. => 2\n\neffectiv