ility_evidence: The wrapper directly provides automatic event logging for high-risk prototypes to meet Article 12’s mandatory record-keeping. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It automates timestamp and input capture but does not ensure logging of risk-trigger events, reference databases or human verifier IDs required by Article 12(2)-(3). Score 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a reusable logging tool yet still treats compliance as an add-on logging practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: A standardized Python decorator/middleware for inference telemetry is common industry practice and not surprising. Score 1.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a shared library and adopting it across projects requires moderate engineering effort but is technically feasible with existing tools. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Automated logging to a secure database aligns with legal requirements and does not offend norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nCheck anchored levels exactly. Ensure evidence one sentence.\n\nPossibly implementability could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Might be easy. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says maintains prototyping speed while ensuring technical viability. I'd lean 3.\n\nAcceptability: logging inputs may raise data privacy concerns but regulation mandates it. Acceptable.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
